Z||000000||Vader||07-07-2000||11:14 PM||||Why am I getting an average of 8,500 miles on a clutch set (clutch plate, pressure plate, TO bearing, and TO lever)? I installed new hardware at 80K, 86K, 101K, and 106K miles. The housing appears to be intact without cracks or distortion, the clutch hub on the transaxle is square with the housing flange, and the flywheel has been machined twice to make sure it's true. The clutch operates smoothly when engaged/released, and there are no unusual noises until the TO bearing starts to go bad. This generally takes the TO lever (fork) with it and anneals the ends of the spring diaphragm to the point where I'm not comfortable re-using it. I have never had a clutch disc wear out and never burned a pressure plate or flywheel surface. <P>The last time I replaced it (last Monday) I found a Bower/BCA bearing that was a replacement for the O.E.M. TO bearing. It cost me about $60.00 as opposed to the $30.00 "bargain" bearing and appears to be better sealed and has a fully ground face on a hardened race compared to a steel "shell" inserted on the face of the cheapo "Hecho en Mejico" bearing.<P>Is this a weakness of the four speed transaxle, or does the Getrag have the same problem? Or am I alone in having poor service from clutches? I have a '50 pickup with a 423 BHP 355 SBC in front of a Borg Super T-10 Aluminum Series IV, and I have a helluva lot more miles on the Borg & Beck clutch with a lot more abuse than the Fiero.<P>Yo - Wassup wif dat, G's? (Any hints would be appreciated.)<P>Later,<BR>Vader<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Make Me Bad</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000001||Vader||07-09-2000||08:59 AM||||No ideas?<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Make Me Bad</I>"||||reg||1
